A||N||2||Eric_PA||HO Quad or 2.8?||||1||
Z||000000||Eric_PA||07-07-2000||12:59 PM||||Hi, this is my first posting and have to ask for advice. I originally had an 84 fiero and was going to swap in a Quad HO. I bought it from Bob at Kick Hill Farms. Before I purchased the kit I found a 861/2 GT with an automatic for a good price. I ended up buying that too. Now I have this Quad laying around and an automatic V6, the GT is going to be converted to stick no matter what. My question is, how stupid would it be for me to swap in the 4 cylinder for the 6? I have a sunfire with the 2.4 and that blows the doors of my automatic fiero. I can't make up my mind on what to do with it. Either stroke the 2.8 and install a manual or put the Quad four in. I lokking for some pros and cons on this.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||07-07-2000||02:30 PM||||Joe Wynman, Associated Auto Inc, did a lot with the Quad 4 conversions. He had some articles written on the conversion. <P>fierojoe@bellatlantic.net <P>If it were me, I'd switch the Quad 4 into the 2M4 and not replace the 2.8 with the Quad 4.  I suspect the swap may be easier.  Plus if you plan to juice up the 2.8 in the GT, you'll have two quick Fieros.||||reg||3
Z||000002||terryk||07-07-2000||05:51 PM||||The Quad 4 is a great rever but not good in the torque arena. For all of the work, I'd go with a 3.4L upgrade or another engine.<P>The Quad 4 cars I've driven (even the HO's) were nice but only after you got them above 3000 RPM. I thought the Duke was better at the lowend but listening to the Q4 wind is a lovely sound.<P>Did you notice the same thing?<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
